It is used to measure very small lengths such as diameter of a wire or thickness of a metal sheet. It has two scales:  


(a) The main scale on the sleeve which has markings of \(0.5 \text{mm}\) each. 
(b) The circular scale on the thimble which has 50 divisions. Some instruments may have main scale marking of \(1 \text{mm}\) and 100 divisions on the thimble.  


When the thimble makes one complete turn, the spindle moves \(0.5 \text{mm}\) (1 scale division) on the main scale which is called pitch of the screw gauge. Thus, its least count is:  


\[\text{Least count} = \frac{\text{Pitch of the screw gauge}}{\text{No. of divisions on the circular scale}} = \frac{0.5 \text{mm}}{50} = 0.01 \text{mm}\]  


