### 1.13 Rounding off the Digits  


When rounding off numbers to a certain number of significant figures, do so to the nearest value. If the last digit is more than 5, the retained digit is increased by one and if it is less than 5, it is retained as such.  


## For example:  


(i) Round off to 2 significant figures: \(2.512 \times 10^3 \text{m}\). Answer: \(2.5 \times 10^3 \text{m}\) (ii) Round off to 3 significant figures: \(3.4567 \times 10^4 \text{kg}\). Answer: \(3.46 \times 10^4 \text{kg}\)  


For the integer 5, there is an arbitrary rule: If the number before the 5 is odd, one is added to the last digit retained. If the number before the 5 is even, it remains the same:

