## Example 2.5  


An iron bob is dropped from the top of a tower. It reaches the ground in 4 seconds. Find: (a) the height of the tower (b) the velocity of the ball as it strikes the ground.  


## Solution  


For freely falling body:  


Initial velocity \(= \nu_{1} = 0\) Acceleration \(= g = 10 \text{m s}^{- 2}\) Time \(= t = 4 \text{s}\) Height (distance) \(= S = h = ?\) Final velocity \(= \nu_{t} = ?\)  


(a) According to second equation of motion,  


\[S = \nu_{1}t + \frac{1}{2} gt^{2}\]  


Putting the values, \(h = 0 \times 4 \text{s} + \frac{1}{2} \times 10 \text{m s}^{- 2} \times (4)^{2} \text{s}^{2}\) \(h = 80 \text{m}\)  


(b) From the first equation of motion, we have  


\[\nu_{t} = \nu_{1} + gt\]  


Putting the values, \(\nu_{t} = 0 + 10 \text{m s}^{- 2} \times 4 \text{s} = 40 \text{m s}^{- 1}\)  


## Example 2.6  


An arrow is thrown vertically upward with the help of a bow. The velocity of the arrow when it leaves the bow is \(30 \text{m s}^{- 1}\) . Determine time to reach the highest point? Also, find the maximum height attained by the arrow.  


## Solution  


Here, acceleration will be taken as negative, for the arrow is thrown vertically upward.  


Initial velocity \(= \nu_{1} = 30 \text{m s}^{- 1}\) Final velocity \(= \nu_{t} = 0\) Acceleration \(= g = - 10 \text{m s}^{- 2}\) Time \(= t = ?\) Height \(S = h = ?\)  


From first equation of motion:  


\[\nu_{t} = \nu_{1} + gt\]  


\[\text{or} \qquad t = \frac{\nu_{1} - \nu_{1}}{-g}\]  


Putting the values \(t = \frac{0 - 30 \text{m s}^{- 1}}{- 10 \text{m s}^{- 2}} = 3 \text{s}\)

