### 4.7 Centre of Gravity and Centre of Mass  


An object is composed of a large number of small particles. Each particle is acted upon by the gravitational force directed towards the centre of the Earth (Fig.4.14- a). As the object is small as compared to the Earth, the value of \(g\) can be taken as uniform over all particles. Therefore, each particle experiences the same force \(mg\) . Since all these forces are parallel and act in the same direction, so their resultant as shown in Fig.4.14(b) will be equal to the sum of all these forces, i.e.  


Resultant force \(= \sum mg\) where \(\sum\) means the "sum of".  

